  The 1500 byte interface MTU is not a mismatch to fix. It describes
  what enp6s0 can put on the wire, and the LAN really does carry that (a
  file copy to another machine on this switch is not limited by the WAN
  path). Lowering it to 1440 would cap every local transfer as well, and
  rule out jumbo frames, to change nothing about the internet path.
  What the 1440 byte limit costs, and how it is already paid for:
    - TCP: nothing, if the router clamps MSS ("Clamp MSS to PMTU"). The
      MSS in every SYN is rewritten, so neither side ever emits an
      oversized segment and the ICMP round trip never happens.
    - Without MSS clamping: one oversized segment and one ICMP reply per
      new connection, after which TCP caches the value per route.
    - UDP/QUIC: does its own discovery regardless of the local MTU.
  Verify clamping is in effect: a fresh HTTPS download should never stall.

